Wonsingi was succeeded by his son [[Jongseo of Kwangju|Jongseo]], who embarked on an international mission to extract promises to defend Kwangju's independence from the [[Age of Imperialism|Imperial powers]] at the time, most famously the ''Duis delegation''. Back home, Jongseo rejected the [[wikipedia:Sadaejuui|Sadaejuui]] of his predecessor, ushering in a phase of Kwangju nationalism known as the [[Plum Blossom Reformation]]. This period involved the procurement of modern warships from [[Andalus]] and [[Mercia]], forming the basis of the [[Yeomyeong fleet]], in an attempt to build up a modern military capable of deterring Sanese aggression, and the appointment of Western advisors to the Imperial Court to guide the political modernization efforts.

[[Anti-Sanese sentiment]] throughout Kwangju continued to grow in the first years of Jongseo's rule, mostly in response to increasingly aggressive Sanese posturing and the increase in Sanese interference in internal Kwangju affairs, along with Jongseo's aggressive nationalistic drive to reject foreign (especially Sanese) encroachment on Kwangju's sovereignty.

===Sanese occupation and resistance===
{{Main|Kōshū|Kwangju independence movement}}
[[File:Provisional Government of the Republic of Korea.jpg|thumb|Early members of the [[Provisional Government of the Kwangju People's Republic]] (1918)]]
With the [[Battle of Jinju|capture of Jinju]] by the [[Imperial Sanese Army]] in 1917, Kwangju was officially annexed into the Mitoyagi Empire, and was subsequently renamed ''Kōshū'', the Sanese term for Kwangju.

The Sanese occupation came to an end on 9 September 1952, when forces of the [[Provisional Government of the Kwangju People's Republic|provisional government]] entered Jinju to accept the surrender of the Sanese garrison, the soldiers were immediately greeted at the city gates by hundreds of thousands of people. The flag of the Mitoyagi Empire was lowered in front of the [[Government-General of Kōshū Building]] and subsequently burned by the soldiers, before being replaced with the flag of People's Republic.

===Liberation and division===
{{Main|Liberation of Kwangju|Division of Kwangju|Kwangju Civil War}}

Immediately following the end of the [[Great Adonian War]], conflicts over how to effectively government Kwangju would come to the forefront of the national dialogue. The provisional government of the [[Kwangju People's Republic]], established in 1918 in Zong by [[Kwangju exiles]], had spearheaded the underground resistance against the Sanese occupation, but was viewed with skepticism by much of the older nobility due to it's predominately [[W:Anarchism|anarchist]] and [[W:Communism|communist]] political orientation.

===== Consolidation of division =====
{{Main|Taegeuk Revolution}}
After the collapse of the provisional government, two rival states emerged: the socialist [[People's Republic of Kwangju]], controlling the western two-thirds of the peninsula, and the [[Republic of Howan]], controlling the remaining eastern third. Howan, an [[W:Autocracy|autocratic]] [[W:Dictatorship|dictatorship]] under the leadership of [[Kim Suk-won]] adopted a strict anti-communist and anti-socialist position, launching bloody purges against and aggressive military policies. Lee Sung Ki also officially ended the war with Seongpo, though he continued to recognize only Howan as the sole legitimate representative of the Kwangju people. Relations between Howan and Seongpo greaty improved, as both had a common mistrust of the Kwangju DPR. In 1965 there was a brief period of warming relations following the exchange of POW's and the reunions of families separated during the civil war. However, this came to an end following a coup d'état in Howan overthrowing the First Republic of Howan, and establishing an autocratic government under the leadership of Seung Wook Yi. Wook-Yi, a strong conservative, embraced economic reforms, and the creation of a large military force. 
[[File:May 18th Movement Archives11.jpg|thumb|left|Pro-democracy student protest, Sejong 1979]]
As a result of Seung Wook Ki's economic policies, Howan's economy grew by an averga of 10% annually, surpassing that of both the Kwangju DPR and Seongpo combined. The rapid export driven growth of Howan's economy was eclisped by the brutal political repression under Seung Wook Ki, eventhough he led the government to develope the nation-wide expressway system, the Taejon subway system, and laid the foundation for the nation's future economic development. The rule of Seung Wook Ki came to an end during the Songdo Uprising, also known as the April 24th Uprising. Student protests who demanded the return of a democratic government to Howan camped out in Taejon's City Square, and staged marches and riots. Over time mounting violence and frustration forced Seung Wook Yi to resign, leading to the establishment of the Third Republic of Howan.

The successful overthrow of the Howanese dictator sparked a series of pro-Democracy demonstrations across the Kwangju Peninsula, marking the beginning of what would be known as the ''Taegeuk Revolution''. The increasingly authoritarian nature of the Seongponese government was met with widespread student protests and demonstrations, following the pattern set by the Howanese Student Democracy Movement. The Prime Minister of Seongpo was overthrown by the Seongponese Armed Forces on 16 June 1979, and free and fair elections were called for 1980, and a new constitution drafted. Similar uprisings occurred in the Kwangju DPR,  but we're brutally crushed by the Kwangju Liberation Army, culminating in the 14 April Massacre. The suppression of the Kwangju Democratic Movement marked the end of the Taegeuk Revolution, and dashed the hopes for a swift reunification for the next three generations.

Tensions between Howan, Kwangju, and Seongpo flared up again after the Kwangju DPR testing its first nuclear weapon on March 18, 1979, under the name "Ganghan". The move was met with condemnation from the international community, and led to the restarting of Howan's nuclear weapons program. Weeks later several hundred Kwangju soldiers crossed the DMZ separating Howan and Kwangju, and attacked several guard stations, killing and injuring hundreds. The nuclear threat posed by the Kwangju DPR forced Howan and Seongpo to dig extensive tunnel systems for defense against a possible nuclear attack. The Kwangju Liberation Army Navy shelled several inhabited islands of Howan and Seongpo, most notably Nonmijŏm Island (Howan), which resulted in the deaths of 12 people, before Howanese ships arrived and chased off the ships.

Howan tested it's first nuclear weapon in 1983 under the name "Donghae", allegedly with help from the Republic of Zong, an allegation in which both Kwangju and Zong both denied. Taejon, Howan hosted the 1992 Summer Adonian Games, which the Kwangju DPR boycotted due to Howan's anti-communist laws and concerns about the safety of its athletes. Howan and Seongpo agreed to march under the flag of a "United Kwangju" in the Parade of Nations, and competed as one team, under the name "United Republic of Kwangju".

