==Government and politics==
{{Politics of Kwangju}}
{{Main|Politics of Kwangju}}{{multiple image|direction=horizontal|align=right|width1=140|width2=140|footer=|image1=Park Ji-hye.png|caption1=[[Park Ji-hye]], [[President of Kwangju]]|image2=Han Ki-Seo.png|caption2=[[Han Ki-Seo]], [[Prime Minister of Kwangju]]}}
Kwangju operates under a [[W:unitary state|unitary system]], as a [[W:Parliamentary republic|parliamentary republic]] with [[W:Universal suffrage|universal suffrage]]. The [[Prime Minister of Kwangju]], the [[W:Head of government|head of government]], is a member of the 300-seat [[National Assembly of Kwangju|National Assembly]], and must maintain the support of a legislative majority ("confidence") in order to remain in office. Usually, the Prime Minister has come from the the largest party in the Assembly. The [[President of Kwangju]] is the ceremonial [[W:Head of state|head of state]], and has no political competencies. Kwangju's current prime minister is [[Han Ki-Seo]], and it's president is [[Park Ji-hye]].

The responsibilities and powers of the government of Kwangju is outlined by the [[Constitution of Kwangju]], which establishes the [[Judiciary of Kwangju|judicial]], [[Council of Ministers (Kwangju)|executive]], and legislative branches of government. The current constitution of Kwangju was adopted in 2018, marking a departure from the provisional basic laws adopted in 2014 which took substantial inspiration from the constitutions [[Constitution of the People's Republic of Kwangju|of the PRK]] and [[Constitution of Howan|Howan]].  

The first direct legislative elections in post-reunification Kwangju took place in [[2015 Kwangju general election|2015]]. Kwangju is presently described as a "[[W:Full democracy|full democracy]]", with [[W:Civil liberties|civil liberties]] such as [[W:Freedom of speech|freedom of speech]], assembly, and [[W:Self-determination|self-determination]] entrenched in its constitution.

=== Administrative divisions ===
Kwangju is divided into 12 provinces (''do''), 1 special autonomous province (''teukbyeol jachido''), 5 metropolitan cities (''gwangyeok-si''), and 1 special city (''teukbyeolsi''). These are further subdivided into a variety of smaller entities, including cities (''si''), counties (''gun''), districts (''gu''), towns (''eup''), townships (''myeon''), neighborhoods (''dong'') and villages (''ri'').

