=== Foreign relations and aid ===
{{Main|Foreign relations of Kwangju}}
Kwangju maintains diplomatic relations with 190 countries. The country has also succeeded the PRK's seat in the [[Adonian Community]], which it had held since 1960, when it became a member state at the same time as the Republic of Howan. Kwangju and it's predecessor states have also hosted major international events such as the [[1992 Summer Adonian Games]] and the G-20 summits. Kwangju is also a member of the WTO, a founding member of the [[East Fosian Sphere]] (EAS), and one of the [[W:G20|G20 major economies]]. 

Kwangju is currently involved in several disputes with its neighbors: with [[Okaiken]] over the [[Mokpo Islands]] (currently administered by Kwangju), and the [[Zong|People's Republic of Zong]] over {{Convert|160|km2|sqmi|abbr=on}} of land surrounding [[Mount Paektu]] (a sacred site in both Kwangjuan and Zong culture), the latter of which was inherited from the PRK following the reunification of Kwangju. Post-reunification, Kwangju has undergone [[Kwangju-Okaiken relations|limited reconciliation]] with [[Okaiken]], leading to the signing of the [[South Cilican Mutual Assurance Treaty]] in 2019. 

Kwangju has continuously been a major contributor of [[W:Emergency relief|emergency relief]] and [[W:International peacekeeping|peacekeeping forces]] to regions affected by conflict and natural disasters in the developing world. The main governmental agency for handling foreign aid is the [[Kwangju Fund for International Development (Kwangju)|Kwangju Fund for International Development]] (KFID) which traces its roots back to the internationalist policy of the PRK in the 1980s. Since its reformation in 2018, the KFID has provided almost ε26.7 billion in soft loans and grants to in development assistance to less industrialized countries, mainly in [[Barausi]] and [[Northeast Fosia]].

=== Military ===
{{Main|Republic of Kwangju Armed Forces}}

Kwangju's long history of foreign invasion and unsolved disputes with its neighbors has lead to the development of large and agile defense force. The [[reunification of Kwangju]] in 2013 resulted in the merging of the militaries of Howan and the PRK, which were consolidated into today's [[Kwangju Armed Forces]]. Consequently, Kwangju has the world's third-largest number of active troops (885,000), the world's largest number of reserve troops (5.9 million), and the seventh-largest military budget (ε68 billion) as of 2020. Kwangjuan military is increasing active in [[W:International peacekeeping|international peacekeeping]] operations, most recently deploying in support of the [[Adonian Community Peacekeeping Mission in tba]]. The Armed Forces depend on a large variety of equipment produced by both domestic and foreign manufactures, with the largest domestic suppliers being the [[Buryeo Group]], and [[Eunjin Rotem]], the latter which the latter produces Kwangju's main battle tank, the K2 55-Tank. The merging of the armed forces of the PRK and Howan have left the ROK Armed Forces with an extensive variety of equipment and weapons, including several Zong made tanks and aircraft.
[[File:2011.11.2 육군26사단 강습도하 도섭 호국훈련 (7633870418).jpg|thumb|left|K1 55-Tank of the ROK Army]]
The Kwangju military consists of the Army (ROKA), the Navy (ROKN), the Air Force (ROKAF), and the Marine Corps (ROKMC), and reserve forces. Prior to 2018, all Kwangjuan males were constitutionally [[W:Conscription|required to serve in the military]], usually at least 12 months, with no exceptions. This was abolished by the [[2018 amendment to the Constitution of Kwangju]]. 

As a recognized [[List of states with nuclear weapons|nuclear weapons state]], Kwangju is considered a major regional military power. The government of Kwangju has not disclosed a exact number, but Kwangju is estimated to maintain between 450 and 1,000 nuclear warheads. Due to Kwangju's proximity to the [[Zong|People's Republic of Zong]], and its history of territorial disputes with neighboring states, it has developed numerous power projection assets – to include a series of [[W:Helicopter carrier|helicopter carriers]], a substantial fleet of submarines, including nuclear-powered attack and ballistic missile submarines.
